Short answer: you don't need the full matrix, but I wouldn't skip real-git testing entirely. A small set of 8–10 real `git commit` cases gives most of the value at a fraction of the cost.

**Why skipping it entirely is risky.** This branch's correctness now rests on three things that only git itself can confirm:
- whether git sets `GIT_EDITOR=:` in each mode;
- the exact shape of the comment block git writes;
- which prefix `auto` picks, and what each cleanup mode keeps.

Every test on the branch simulates those. I checked `GIT_EDITOR` once by hand on git 2.50, but nothing re-checks it on CI's git version or catches a later regression. The two real bugs this review found (an exported `GIT_EDITOR=:`, and `auto` read two ways) sit exactly in that gap, as do A, B and C from earlier rounds. Simulated tests can't catch that class, because they make the same assumptions as the code.

**What you can skip.** The full matrix (8 modes × 7 configs × 4 message shapes, about 220 cells) is more than this PR needs. Each assumption needs one case that hits it:
- `-m` with a `#` subject, and with a body ending in a `#` paragraph;
- `-m -e -v` under `auto`;
- a plain editor commit;
- `--amend` with the editor, and `--amend --no-edit`;
- `-C HEAD`;
- `commit.status=false`;
- one cleared message, which must abort.

Each case asserts the same three things: the final block names exactly the stamped ID, the user's lines are unchanged, and a cleared message aborts.

**It's cheap here.** `redo_commit_index_test.go` already has `gitWithEntireCommitHooks`, which runs real `git commit` with hooks pointing at the test-built binary. It only needs a `commit-msg` hook and a scripted `GIT_EDITOR` added. That's a few hours of work, not a new harness.

If you'd rather keep this PR's scope fixed, the alternative is to merge with the gap stated plainly in the PR body and land the real-git test right after. Since every round so far has found something in this seam, I'd add the small version before merge. The proposal card from my last message covers the big matrix; if you want the small version instead, tell me and I'll do it here.

